{<b>Exercise Eight Answers</b>}
[BCFlags "17"]

[Event ""]
[Date ""]
[Board "8-1"]
[West ""]
[North ""]
[East ""]
[South ""]
[Dealer "N"]
[Vulnerable "None"]
[Deal ""]
[Scoring ""]
[Declarer ""]
[Contract ""]
[Result ""]
{<b>8-1)</b> <i>Two tricks. Play the suit twice, driving out both the ace and king to
promote to winners. Start with the queen, higher card from the shorter side.</i>}
[BCFlags "17"]

[Event ""]
[Date ""]
[Board "8-2"]
[West ""]
[North ""]
[East ""]
[South ""]
[Dealer "N"]
[Vulnerable "None"]
[Deal ""]
[Scoring ""]
[Declarer ""]
[Contract ""]
[Result ""]
{<b>8-2)</b> <i>Four tricks. The opponents five cards are likely to be divided 3-2. Play
the ace, then duck a round (to conserve entries and see how the cards lie).
Upon regaining the lead, play the king. The last two small cards in the West
hand will be length winners.</i>}
[BCFlags "17"]

[Event ""]
[Date ""]
[Board "8-3"]
[West ""]
[North ""]
[East ""]
[South ""]
[Dealer "N"]
[Vulnerable "None"]
[Deal ""]
[Scoring ""]
[Declarer ""]
[Contract ""]
[Result ""]
{<b>8-3)</b> <i>Three tricks. You can use the queen to drive out the king, promoting the
jack into a second trick. Better, if you have the entries, it to lead toward
the queen. If the queen wins, return to the East hand and play to the jack. If
South holds the king, you take three tricks via the repeated finesse.</i>}
[BCFlags "17"]

[Event ""]
[Date ""]
[Board "8-4"]
[West ""]
[North ""]
[East ""]
[South ""]
[Dealer "N"]
[Vulnerable "None"]
[Deal ""]
[Scoring ""]
[Declarer ""]
[Contract ""]
[Result ""]
{<b>8-4)</b> <i>Three tricks. Lead a low card toward the queen. If North holds the king,
and if the suit divides 3-2, you will take three tricks through a combination
of finessing and length.</i>}
[BCFlags "17"]

[Event ""]
[Date ""]
[Board "8-5"]
[West ""]
[North ""]
[East ""]
[South ""]
[Dealer "N"]
[Vulnerable "None"]
[Deal ""]
[Scoring ""]
[Declarer ""]
[Contract ""]
[Result ""]
{<b>8-5)</b> <i>Four tricks. Lead a low card toward the queen. If the queen wins, duck a</i>
<i>round. If the suit divides 3-2, the ace will now pick up the last outstanding</i>
<i>card, giving you two more tricks by length.</i>}
[BCFlags "17"]
